        "starter": "import numpy as np\n\ndef euler(f, t0, y0, h, n):\n    \"\"\"n Euler steps of y' = f(t, y). Returns arrays t and y.\n    t has n + 1 entries; y[k] is the value (a number or a vector) at t[k].\"\"\"\n    ...\n\ndef observed_order(errors, hs):\n    \"\"\"Slope of log(error) against log(h), by a straight-line fit.\"\"\"\n    ...\n",
        "solution": "import numpy as np\n\ndef euler(f, t0, y0, h, n):\n    \"\"\"n Euler steps of y' = f(t, y). Returns arrays t and y.\n    t has n + 1 entries; y[k] is the value (a number or a vector) at t[k].\"\"\"\n    t = t0 + h * np.arange(n + 1)\n    y = np.zeros((n + 1,) + np.shape(y0))\n    y[0] = y0\n    for k in range(n):\n        y[k + 1] = y[k] + h * np.asarray(f(t[k], y[k]))\n    return t, y\n\ndef observed_order(errors, hs):\n    \"\"\"Slope of log(error) against log(h), by a straight-line fit.\"\"\"\n    return float(np.polyfit(np.log(hs), np.log(errors), 1)[0])\n",
