  "summary": {
    "ideas": [
      "Euler follows the tangent: $y_{n+1} = y_n + hf(t_n, y_n)$. On $y' = ry$ it is compounding.",
      "Its error at a fixed time is proportional to $h$: halve the step and the error halves. That is first order.",
      "On $y' = -\\lambda y$ it is stable only for $h < 2/\\lambda$; past that the steps flip sign and grow."
    ],
    "formula": {
      "name": "Euler's method",
      "tex": "y_{n+1} = y_n + h\\,f(t_n, y_n), \\qquad \\text{stable on } y' = -\\lambda y \\text{ for } h < \\tfrac{2}{\\lambda}",
      "note": "Global error proportional to $h$."
    },
    "trap": "A step past $2/\\lambda$ makes a decaying solution blow up, with alternating signs."
  },
